 here is that jason kenney's  struggles managing the pandemic  are starting to uh impact federal vote  intentions as well  and the ndp being in second place  suggests that uh rachel naughtley within  the province is having  a positive effect on the fortune of the  ndp  uh federally as well the trend in  alberta though is the same in manitoba  and saskatchewan  torrey's support is way down from the  2019 election  but in ontario and quebec ipsos found  voters in those provinces  would make the same choices now that  they made in 2019  consider seat rich ontario the liberals  have a commanding lead at  40 right now but that's pretty much what  they won in 2019  41 the conservatives have 32 percent  support in ontario right now versus  33 in 2019 the ndp though is moving up  polling at 20 now versus 17  in 2019. yeah there's not a lot of  movement in ontario it's pretty well  status quo from wherever we were in  2019.  now if you're justin trudeau trying to  figure out if you should have a summer  election  the liberals would almost certainly win  the most seats but the numbers are not  quite there for the liberals to bank on  winning  enough seats to form the majority  government that they want  jeff david aiken in ottawa thanks david  still ahead how the fight against covet  19 left behind one of canada's most  vulnerable communities  a fishy fixture of toronto tourism is  back in business  the city's popular ripley's aquarium  finally welcomed back  guests with masks today after shutting  its doors for eight months due to the  pandemic  the aquarium joins a slew of tourist  attractions that reopened across ontario  this weekend  including the nearby cn tower it comes  one week after the province entered step  three  of its pandemic reopening plan and the  covet 19 pandemic  uprooted life for many canadians but for  those living with intellectual  disabilities the last 18 months carried  extraordinary challenges and a higher  risk of infection  as jamie morocco explains the community  says it's been left  behind corey earl has an intellectual  disability and says the pandemic  stripped him of his routine his social  circle and his support  disabilities have been looked over  beyond this pandemic  we certainly have been left behind the  results of a large scale study out of  the uk last week show earl may be right  it found adults living with intellectual  disabilities face an  increased risk of hospitalization or  death due to kova 19.  three times that of the general  population people with down syndrome or  cerebral palsy were most in jeopardy  because of pre-existing conditions  including obesity diabetes epilepsy and  even poverty  laura lachance's 28 year old son kevin  has down syndrome  it was a challenge to get him to  increase his social distancing  and to be very careful about hand  sanitizing and to wear a mask in public  places  bashan says since she was able to care  for him at home  he wasn't isolated and was given an  education on vaccination  but across the country covet 19 shot  prioritization differs from province to  province  they may have been priorized but  absolutely people are going to need help  in getting access to it whether that's  online  whether that's physically getting there  etc  bioethicist carrie bowman says the  pandemic has highlighted the  marginalization of our community's most  vulnerable  and wants to see the government develop  systems  of access to support safety  as well as potentially vaccines people  were not treated  well at all uh in many many cases  during this pandemic and you know they  weren't prioritized for personal  protective equipment  their workers weren't like prioritized  there were all kinds of issues  for people with disabilities across the  country experts we spoke with say  unlike in the uk canada's not currently  tracking data when it comes to the  mortality rate of intellectually  disabled amid covet  however they have no reason to believe  the statistics would be any different  adding without further financial and  healthcare support  more people are at risk jamie morocco  global news toronto  up next the new sporting events making  waves at the summer olympics  welcome back when it comes to the summer  olympics we often think about the  traditional  big events like the 100 meter dash or  gymnastics  but that doesn't mean the canadian  athletes aren't open to learning new  tricks  as mike delay reports the tokyo olympics  are offering up some fresh fare  and hoping to take the games to new  heights  if you talk to most sport climbers the  olympics were the furthest thing from  their mind  when they took up the sport when it was  announced in 2016 that climbing would be  added for the tokyo games  canada's best briefly celebrated and  then chalked up and got to work  it's dynamic it's you know it's exciting  it's  it's fast it's it's fun the olympics has  a history of trying out new sports  some like ballooning and tug of war  didn't catch on  baseball and softball were dropped after  the 2008 games for being too  american but the international community  has caught up enough  that both sports are back in tokyo sadly  few fans will see either live and in  person  because of covet regulations but the  players say and cue the sports cliche  they're just happy to be here when we do  get to the games that i think will look  very different than they have in the  past but at the end of the day  it's about the product on the field and  the teams competing for a medal for  their country  fans no fans spectacle opening  ceremonies are not like it's it's about  that competition in those games so i  think that's what we're  we're most excited for other sports  making their debuts  karate surfing and skateboarding  sports that have massive followings and  established communities  yet are only now making their olympic  debuts it really doesn't hit until you  stop  and think about it like every once in a  while i'll sit down with someone and  i'll talk about it  wow that is insane that this is the  first time  ever this is going to be included in the  olympics all the new sport athletes say  they've thought about how they'll  represent their disciplines to the world  they want an audience and medals too of  course  as far as the crowd goes i'm i i could  say i'm fairly bummed  but it's not it's not like i'm going to  the olympics bro that  it's hard to find a downside in that  totally bro  mike drew like global news toronto  luck out there bro and that is global  national for this saturday night  i'm jeff semple tonight your canada is  peterborough ontario  we would love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending us those  photos  to viewers at globalnational.com thank  you for watching and i'll be back here  with you tomorrow  have a great night  you
 

